WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.320
A kidney tubule pulls sodium back all along its length.

2
00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:08.660
Close one sodium door near the middle, and more potassium leaves in the urine,

3
00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:11.800
farther down. Why potassium?

4
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:17.720
Filtered fluid flows along the tube.

5
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.340
Each stretch takes sodium back through its own door.

6
00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:26.080
The door sits on the apical side, the side facing the fluid.

7
00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:29.370
Every door runs on the same engine.

8
00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:35.930
On the basolateral side, facing the surrounding tissue, the sodium-potassium pump

9
00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:40.930
exports sodium. That keeps sodium low inside, so it flows in.

10
00:00:41.933 --> 00:00:43.853
Along the tube, the doors change.

11
00:00:45.193 --> 00:00:49.133
The proximal tubule uses NHE3 and SGLT2.

12
00:00:50.273 --> 00:00:53.153
The thick ascending limb uses NKCC2.

13
00:00:54.333 --> 00:00:57.033
The distal convoluted tubule uses NCC.

14
00:00:58.273 --> 00:01:00.213
The collecting duct uses ENaC.

15
00:01:01.383 --> 00:01:03.083
They take very different shares.

16
00:01:04.463 --> 00:01:10.203
In rounded human estimates, the proximal tubule takes back about 65% % of filtered

17
00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:13.603
sodium. The thick limb takes about 25.

18
00:01:14.923 --> 00:01:19.803
The distal convoluted tubule takes about 5, and the collecting duct about 3.

19
00:01:20.867 --> 00:01:25.667
Each door brings sodium in its own way, so a different drug can block each one.

20
00:01:26.967 --> 00:01:31.387
Furosemide, a loop diuretic, blocks NKCC2 in the thick limb.

21
00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:36.830
Block that door, and up to about a quarter of filtered sodium flows on.

22
00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:42.710
The segments downstream normally take back only about 8% % between them.

23
00:01:43.717 --> 00:01:49.757
That extra sodium flows on to ENaC, in the late distal tubule and collecting duct.

24
00:01:50.697 --> 00:01:54.757
And ENaC lets sodium in alone, with no partner ion.

25
00:01:55.850 --> 00:02:00.250
So positive charge is leaving the fluid, with nothing to balance it.

26
00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:03.790
What happens to the charge of the fluid left behind?

27
00:02:09.867 --> 00:02:16.387
It goes negative. That negative lumen drives potassium out of the cell and into the

28
00:02:16.387 --> 00:02:21.087
urine. It leaves through a potassium channel called ROMK.

29
00:02:22.167 --> 00:02:23.667
Here is the one idea.

30
00:02:25.007 --> 00:02:29.627
Every segment has its own sodium door, and every door runs on one pump.

31
00:02:30.927 --> 00:02:34.687
Block an upstream door, and the sodium it misses reaches ENaC.

32
00:02:35.627 --> 00:02:37.627
More potassium is secreted.

33
00:02:38.517 --> 00:02:42.837
How much potassium depends on how much extra sodium and flow arrive.

34
00:02:44.037 --> 00:02:48.277
And aldosterone, raised by sustained salt loss, adds to the effect.

35
00:02:49.383 --> 00:02:52.463
So a sodium door is never only about sodium.

36
00:02:53.683 --> 00:02:58.763
Whatever it misses, the tube downstream has to handle, and potassium pays the price.
